#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:59
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:61
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>&marble; logo</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:64
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>&marble; logo</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:67
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Welcome to &marble;, a small interactive globe and geographical atlas that "
"puts the world at your fingertips. Just like a real atlas or a conventional "
"globe &marble; allows you to freely move across its map and lookup places. "
"Furthermore &marble; allows you to zoom in and have many different views on "
"the surface of the Earth. In its default configuration &marble; offers three "
"different views: <guilabel>Atlas</guilabel>, <guilabel>Satellite View</"
"guilabel> and <guilabel>Earth at Night</guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&marble; comes with a small database of more than 12,000 locations (cities, "
"mountains, volcanoes) which can be searched for and which are partially "
"integrated with the popular Internet encyclopedia Wikipedia. Additionally "
"you can measure distances using &marble; between multiple measure points "
"which can be set freely."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&marble; is free software and licensed under the &GNU; Lesser General Public "
"License."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; quick start guide: Navigation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here is &marble; the first time you run it, either from the K menu-"
">Edutainment->Science->marble or with <keycombo action=\"simul\">&Alt;"
"<keycap>F2</keycap></keycombo> and entering <command>marble</command> into "
"the field."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:90
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>&marble; main window</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:93
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>&marble; main window</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On the right you can see a topographical map of our beautiful home planet. "
"To allow for better orientation the map offers a scale bar in the lower left "
"corner as well as a windrose on the top right. To navigate and to control "
"the view you can use the toolbox on the left: Press the arrow buttons to "
"rotate the globe. The <guilabel>Top</guilabel> and <guilabel>Bottom</"
"guilabel> arrow buttons will tilt the earth axis back and forth. The "
"<guilabel>Left</guilabel> and <guilabel>Right</guilabel> arrow buttons will "
"make the earth spin around its physical axis."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:102
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can accomplish the same behaviour by pressing the &LMB; somewhere on the "
"globe and by moving the mouse while keeping the &LMB; pressed. Using this "
"drag and drop style navigation will allow you to adjust the viewing angle "
"much more easily and more precisely. The cursor keys on your keyboard offer "
"another alternative way to quickly change directions."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:105
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Zoom in and out by moving the vertical slider up and down. If your mouse has "
"got a mouse wheel you can use that one instead - or you just press the &LMB; "
"and the &RMB; down both at once while moving the mouse up and down. Changing "
"the zoom level step by step can be done via the <guilabel>Zoom In</guilabel> "
"and <guilabel>Zoom Out</guilabel> buttons which are placed above and below "
"the zoom slider (or using the <keycap>+</keycap> and <keycap>-</keycap> keys "
"on your keyboard."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Depending on the map's resolution zooming in will provide you with more "
"detail. Smaller cities will appear and using the topographic map you might "
"notice that coastlines are provided as vector graphics."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In case you should get lost you can always reset the viewing angle and zoom "
"level back to the point where we started off: Just hit the <guilabel>Home</"
"guilabel> button (or the <keycap>Home</keycap> button on your keyboard)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid "Choosing different map views for &marble;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:120
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&marble; comes with three different views: <guilabel>Atlas</guilabel>, "
"<guilabel>Satellite View</guilabel> and <guilabel>Earth at Night</guilabel>. "
"You can choose among these by pressing the map view tab on the bottom of "
"&marble;'s toolbox:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; map views"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:128
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; Map Views"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:134
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Topographical map</guilabel>: A classic topographic map. It uses "
"vector lines ( \"MicroWorldDataBase II\" ) to mark coastlines, country "
"borders &etc; and bitmap graphics ( \"ETOPO2\" ) to create the height relief."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:139
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Satellite View</guilabel>: Earth as seen from Space. The map is "
"based on NASA's beautiful \"Blue Marble Next Generation\" pictures. Credits: "
"NASA’s Earth Observatory"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:144
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Earth at Night</guilabel>: This image of Earth’s city lights was "
"created with data from the Defense Meteorological Satellite Program (DMSP) "
"Operational Linescan System (OLS)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:152
#, no-c-format
msgid "Searching places using &marble;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:153
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&marble; comes with a small database of more than 12,000 cities ( from "
"http://www.worldgazetteer.com ) and a few mountains and volcanoes. You can "
"find a location by entering its name into the search line on the top of the "
"toolbox. If you're unsure about the spelling (&marble; always uses the "
"native name in latin letters) you can choose the location browser below the "
"search line as well: A double click on the respective entry will instantly "
"display the matching place on the map."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; searching places"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; Searching Places"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:167
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Once you found your city on the map you can click on its label or its symbol "
"using the &LMB;. A popup menu will appear which allows you to retrieve more "
"information about the city. This popup menu can also be used to find out the "
"coordinates for any point on the globe as the coordinates always get "
"displayed at the bottom of the popup menu. After clicking the name of the "
"location on the popup menu there will appear a data sheet dialog like this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:175
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; data sheet"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:178
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; Data Sheet"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:181
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On the first tab of the dialog very basic data is provided, like coordinates "
"and the name and flag of the country the place belongs to. Additionally "
"population numbers for cities get shown as well as the elevation for "
"mountains. If your computer is connected to the Internet and if you're "
"online &marble; will try to connect to the popular Internet encyclopedia "
"<quote>Wikipedia</quote>. If a matching Wikipedia article is available "
"&marble; will display it on the second tab of the dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:188
#, no-c-format
msgid "Measuring distances with &marble;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:189
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As mentioned already &marble; always displays a dynamic scale bar on the "
"lower left to estimate distances on the map. Together with the windrose in "
"the top right corner these overlays are provided for better orientation. But "
"there's more: &marble; allows you to measure distances between two or more "
"points on earth. To do so click the respective points in correct order on "
"the globe using the &RMB;. On each click a popup menu will appear which "
"allows you to add a measure point (<guilabel>Add Measure Point</guilabel>) "
"or to remove all measure points altogether (<guilabel>Remove Measure Points</"
"guilabel>):"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:195
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; measuring"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:198
#, no-c-format
msgid "&marble; measuring distances"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:201
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Once you have added at least two measure points the total distance will be "
"displayed in the top left corner of the map. &marble; will assume a "
"spherical earth for all measurements which should be accurate enough for "
"most cases."
msgstr ""

